{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791043488,"nonce":"Up3jGO1d8okzwAVE1XljCGcEXT0=","title":"clip-path has no fill rule, and the renderer silently assumes nonzero","message":"`ClipPath::Polygon { points }` and `ClipPath::Path { vertices }` carry no\nfill rule, so a renderer cannot tell which one a clip asked for. CSS\nspells it `polygon(evenodd, 50% 0, …)` and `path(evenodd, \"M…\")`, with\nnonzero as the default.\n\n**Which rule Blinc uses today: nonzero.** `calculate_polygon_clip_alpha`\naccumulates a winding number and tests it against zero. That answers the\nquestion the ashui session asked; it was not written down anywhere.\n\nRequested by the ashui session, which draws all seven `ClipPath` shapes\nin its own shaders and follows them in its hit test. Its CSS reader\nrefuses `evenodd` for now, because the value has nowhere to go, so it is\nnot blocked — it fails cleanly.\n\n\n1. A `FillRule { NonZero, EvenOdd }` in blinc_core, `Default` as\n   `NonZero`, and the field on both variants. Small.\n2. The CSS parser accepting a leading `evenodd` or `nonzero` keyword in\n   `polygon()` and `path()`. Additive; it currently accepts neither.\n3. An encoding to get the rule to the GPU. The polygon clip travels as\n   `CLIP_POLYGON` (clip type 4) with a `vertex_count` and an\n   `aux_offset`. There is no spare field, so the rule has to go\n   somewhere — a high bit of `vertex_count` is the obvious candidate,\n   or a second clip type.\n4. `calculate_polygon_clip_alpha` honouring it. **That function is\n   duplicated across twelve shader files in two variants, eight of one\n   and four of the downlevel one.** Compare the nine identical copies of\n   `sd_shaped_rect` that 2ad76a9 had to be applied to.\n\nStep 4 is the bulk of it, and the duplication is the reason. Worth\nconsidering whether these shared SDF helpers should be generated or\nincluded from one source before another change has to touch all twelve\nby hand.","files":null}]}